# Contributing to Codexeron

First of all, thank you for your interest in contributing to Codexeron.

We welcome contributions from developers around the world. Whether you're fixing a bug, improving documentation, adding a feature, or suggesting an enhancement, your help is greatly appreciated.

---

# Before You Start

Please ensure that you:

- Read the project documentation.
- Search existing Issues before opening a new one.
- Follow the project's coding standards.
- Keep Pull Requests focused on a single purpose.

---

# Development Environment

Recommended tools:

- PHP 8.3+
- Composer
- MySQL 8+
- Git
- Visual Studio Code or PhpStorm

Optional:

- Docker
- Redis

---

# Branch Strategy

Please use the following naming conventions:

feature/feature-name

bugfix/issue-name

hotfix/security-fix

docs/update-documentation

refactor/component-name

Example:

feature/product-review-system

---

# Commit Message Convention

Use clear and descriptive commit messages.

Examples:

feat: add product rating system

fix: resolve SQL injection vulnerability

docs: update installation guide

refactor: improve authentication service

style: format source code

test: add unit tests

---

# Coding Standards

Please follow:

- PSR-12 Coding Style
- Meaningful variable names
- Small reusable methods
- Proper comments where necessary
- Security-first development

Avoid:

- Duplicate code
- Hardcoded secrets
- Unused files
- Dead code

---

# Pull Request Guidelines

Before submitting:

- Update documentation if necessary.
- Test your changes.
- Resolve merge conflicts.
- Ensure no sensitive data is committed.

Every Pull Request should clearly explain:

- What changed
- Why it changed
- How it was tested

---

# Reporting Bugs

Please include:

- PHP version
- Browser
- Operating System
- Steps to reproduce
- Expected behavior
- Actual behavior
- Screenshots (if applicable)

---

# Feature Requests

Feature requests should explain:

- Problem
- Proposed solution
- Alternatives considered
- Benefits

---

# Security

Security vulnerabilities must NOT be reported through public GitHub Issues.

Please see SECURITY.md.

---

# Code Review

Every contribution may be reviewed for:

- Code quality
- Security
- Performance
- Maintainability
- Documentation

Maintainers may request changes before merging.

---

# Documentation

Documentation improvements are always welcome.

This includes:

- Tutorials
- Examples
- Installation guides
- API documentation
- FAQ

---

# Community

Please follow our Code of Conduct.

Be respectful.

Be constructive.

Help others learn.

---

# License

By contributing, you agree that your contributions will be licensed under the project's license.

---

# Thank You

Thank you for helping build Codexeron into a trusted platform for developers worldwide.
